# RAG-Anything unified web deployment plan (issues #19–#26)

## Target architecture

One FastAPI/uvicorn process owns the public application.  It serves the built
single-page frontend, the existing RAG-Anything API, and (when available) the
LightRAG WebUI.  There is no authentication, login route, session state, or
separate Node runtime in production.

```
api/
  main.py                 # app factory/lifespan; API routers; static SPA; /lightrag mount
  config.py               # single environment-backed runtime configuration
  deps.py                 # creates the one RAGAnything/LightRAG-backed service
  routers/
    documents.py          # upload, list, status and delete
    query.py              # text and multimodal query contracts
  services/
    rag.py                # shared RAG facade and LightRAG-compatible configuration
    query.py              # query validation/adaptation and answer/citation streaming
  middleware/
    lightrag_write_guard.py # blocks LightRAG text-only write endpoints
web/
  package.json, vite.config.*  # reproducible production build under web/dist
  src/
    main.* and router.*        # /documents and /chat routes; / redirects to /documents
    api/                       # shared fetch, SSE and upload clients plus DTOs
    pages/documents/           # ingest UI, polling, errors and capability indicators
    pages/chat/                # modes, image upload, streaming answers and citations
    components/                # navigation (including knowledge graph), shared UI/styles
tests/api/                # request/schema/config and LightRAG-mount/guard tests
tests/e2e/                # browser flow with a fixture multimodal PDF
```

The root static handler serves the SPA entry for client routes and sends `/` to
the documents screen; it must not retain the current marketing HTML, auth
redirect, or its static-hosting exception.  Assets are served without an
Origin gate.  Proxy-aware public URLs/origin checks use `X-Forwarded-Host` and
`X-Forwarded-Proto` (or `ALLOWED_CORS_ORIGIN`), never the rewritten `Host`.

## Shared contracts and configuration

- Keep document lifecycle endpoints as the source of truth: upload accepts a
  file and returns its server-managed document/artifact path; `GET
  /documents/{id}/status` provides state/error for polling.
- Define multimodal content around `img_path`.  The chat client uploads its
  image first, receives a server-accessible path, then posts it to `POST
  /query/multimodal`.  The backend accepts legacy `image_data` only through an
  explicit compatibility/validation branch and returns stable 4xx errors for
  empty, missing, inaccessible, or malformed fields.
- `Settings` is the one environment source.  Map its working directory,
  parser/output settings, LLM, vision and embedding settings into
  `RAGAnythingConfig` and the LightRAG WebUI/server creation so all use one
  persistent LightRAG store, vectors and graph.  Retire or alias the duplicate
  `WORKING_DIR`/`OUTPUT_DIR`/`PARSER` environment paths rather than letting
  them drift from `RAG_*` settings.
- Frontend API DTOs mirror the FastAPI payloads.  The shared client normalizes
  API errors and parses typed SSE `delta`, `citations`, `done`, and `error`
  events.  Citations retain source/document information for display.
- The LightRAG integration is optional at runtime: probe package/static assets
  during lifespan/startup.  If absent, do not render the knowledge-graph link
  and report `lightrag_webui: false` in `/healthz`; do not expose a broken 404
  route.  If present, mount under `/lightrag` with its static base/API prefix
  rewritten for that subpath.
- `/lightrag` is read-only in this deployment.  Hide its import controls where
  supported; independently intercept its document insertion/upload/write
  methods in middleware and return a clear response directing users to the
  main documents screen.

## Delivery order and issue dependencies

1. **#19** Remove the landing page/auth redirect and make `/` enter document
   management.  This clears the prerequisite for the SPA.
2. **#20** Introduce the maintained `web/` project, shared client/routing,
   Chinese responsive Documents and Chat UI, then build it into FastAPI static
   assets.  It depends on #19.
3. **#21** Replace browser base64 `image_data` posting with upload-plus-
   `img_path`; add backend compatibility validation and API tests.  It depends
   on #20.
4. **#22** Consolidate API/library/LightRAG storage and model configuration,
   document all variables in `.env.example`.  This is required before sharing
   the graph UI.
5. **#23** Mount the verified optional LightRAG WebUI at `/lightrag`, including
   a capability-aware frontend navigation entry.  It depends on #22.
6. **#24** Apply the UI suppression and server-side write guard to that mount.
   It depends on #23.
7. **#25** Document/build the single-uvicorn deployment, Python 3.10+ and
   optional MinerU/LibreOffice requirements.  It depends on #20 and #23.
8. **#26** Run browser/API E2E coverage for no-auth root access, multimodal PDF
   upload to `ready`, cited chat answer, graph visibility, and absence of the
   former `invalid_request` auth behavior.  It depends on #21, #24 and #25.

## Key checks

Build the frontend before deployment (no development server); start only
`uvicorn api.main:app`.  API tests mock RAG/LightRAG availability, file paths,
and streaming.  E2E uses a fixture PDF with both image and table and skips or
marks the graph assertion only when `/healthz` explicitly declares the optional
WebUI unavailable.
